WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.356 --> 00:00:03.612
- Coral's kind of like
the trees of a forest.

00:00:03.612 --> 00:00:07.175
They're just the backbone of
the whole tropical ecosystem.

00:00:07.175 --> 00:00:11.258
And if they disappear,
we're in a lot of trouble.

00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:15.320
My name's Ken Nedimyer, I'm the founder

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:19.147
of the Coral Restoration Foundation.

00:00:19.147 --> 00:00:20.058
The reason I'm doing this,

00:00:20.058 --> 00:00:23.095
I was, I’d grown up diving, spent
a lot of time in the water.

00:00:23.095 --> 00:00:24.346
One, two, three.

00:00:24.346 --> 00:00:26.720
(splashing)

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:28.794
I've basically watched
the coral reefs dying.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:31.330
As the reefs died, the
fish didn't come back,

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.256
and I realized, I'm
tired of watching it die,

00:00:33.256 --> 00:00:35.084
I need to do something about it.

00:00:35.084 --> 00:00:38.358
And so I developed this
whole idea of growing corals

00:00:38.358 --> 00:00:42.960
in an offshore nursery and
replanting them on the reef.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.128
- [Man] And so you're
kind of like a farmer?

00:00:45.128 --> 00:00:49.427
- I'm a farmer. (laughing)

00:00:49.427 --> 00:00:51.140
Yeah, we grow corals just like a farm.

00:00:51.140 --> 00:00:52.637
There's a good time of year to plant,

00:00:52.637 --> 00:00:54.358
and there's a harvest time.

00:00:54.358 --> 00:00:57.847
And we have five offshore
nurseries in the Keys.

00:00:57.847 --> 00:00:59.602
Each tree can hold 100

00:00:59.602 --> 00:01:00.435
corals.

00:01:01.441 --> 00:01:03.611
We start with little
fragments that we collect.

00:01:03.611 --> 00:01:06.125
And after six to nine
months, maybe a year,

00:01:06.125 --> 00:01:08.863
that fragment has turned into a colony

00:01:08.863 --> 00:01:11.323
that might have 100
centimeters of growth on it.

00:01:11.323 --> 00:01:15.490
We cut that off, and then
we plant it out on the reef.

00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:19.246
So we just harvested some corals,

00:01:19.246 --> 00:01:20.897
and we're gonna take
'em out to Pickles Reef

00:01:20.897 --> 00:01:22.702
right now and plant them.

00:01:22.702 --> 00:01:24.425
We've got a couple
thousand of 'em out there

00:01:24.425 --> 00:01:28.928
that we've already planted
and we're gonna add some more.

00:01:28.928 --> 00:01:30.972
It is a bit of gardening
when you put the corals

00:01:30.972 --> 00:01:32.255
back out on the reef you're saying,

00:01:32.255 --> 00:01:33.774
“Ah, I think they would
look nice over there,

00:01:33.774 --> 00:01:35.539
and that would look nice over there;” and,

00:01:35.539 --> 00:01:38.157
some of it's based on
what used to be there

00:01:38.157 --> 00:01:41.968
and what should be there, and
that's how we do our planting.

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:44.365
We planted 20,000 already this year.

00:01:44.365 --> 00:01:46.247
We'll plant 25,000 next year.

00:01:46.247 --> 00:01:49.231
Those corals will
probably spawn next year.

00:01:49.231 --> 00:01:51.742
Part of the long-range
goal is to get the corals

00:01:51.742 --> 00:01:54.568
reproducing on their own.

00:01:54.568 --> 00:01:56.410
I'm excited every time I get in the water,

00:01:56.410 --> 00:01:57.905
whether I'm going to work in the nursery

00:01:57.905 --> 00:02:00.752
or whether I'm planting corals or just

00:02:00.752 --> 00:02:02.730
looking for new areas to plant.

00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:04.410
A lot of people said, “Oh
you can never do that,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:06.654
it'll never work, can't do
it on a big enough scale;”

00:02:06.654 --> 00:02:09.414
and I think I've proven
that it can be done.

00:02:09.414 --> 00:02:11.960
And if we can train enough
people and teach enough people

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:14.528
in other places, I think we can really see

00:02:14.528 --> 00:02:16.314
a significant turnaround.

00:02:16.314 --> 00:02:19.062
Another day, another coral.

00:02:19.062 --> 00:02:21.645
(mellow piano)

